  "encounterTable": "route_6",
  "_design": "The first route in the game where the ground is not the road. Everywhere else in Caelora the player walks on whatever happens to be under them and the path is a suggestion; here the peat is walkable but dead slow to read, the marsh has to be waded, the lagoon is shut, and the only continuous dry line from one end to the other is a plank walk that somebody built and somebody has to maintain. That inversion is the design, and every beat on the route is about the state of that infrastructure.",
  "_plan": "Three decisions, in order. FIRST the fog: it closes sight to about four tiles, which means a player cannot read the shape of this map from one screen and never will -- so the map is not laid out to be read, it is laid out to be FOLLOWED. Lantern to lantern on the plank, glowcap to glowcap off it. Both are ordinary tiles and the fog reads them straight off the map with nothing scripted, so a player who works out that orange is people and green is mire has been taught the whole region in one screen. SECOND the broken span: the direct line south is out from row 31 to row 35, and there are two honest answers -- wade five tiles of open marsh with every encounter in it, or walk twenty-six tiles of dry plank round the east. Neither is correct. THIRD the lagoon: eleven rows of deep water in plain sight, an islet in the middle of it, and something on the islet. It is on the way to nothing, the sign says so, and it stays shut until the player can swim. That is the reason to come back, said once and not laboured.",
